| 2 | 1000 | 1000 | reject | terminal |

Actual left-edge intersection (0,1.5); visible=false.
\image(figures/cohen_inconclusive.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The left cut produces a point above the viewport, acquiring a top bit. AND zero was an instruction to continue, rather than proof of a visible fragment.)
\alttext(A grey diagonal misses a blue unit square above its upper-left corner. The computed x-zero intersection at y 1.5 and the remaining segment are red, both above the square.)

The stronger invariant concerns whole half-planes. Once an edge has been
processed, both remaining endpoints satisfy its closed half-plane: one is on
the edge and the other was already on its inner side. Every subsequent
replacement lies between those current endpoint coordinates, so that half-plane
stays satisfied. A processed edge cannot be selected again. Four different
edges give at most four cuts and one final mask decision, with constant worst-case
work and storage per input segment. This is a worst-case bound, not an amortized
claim. It does not rank elapsed speed against other clipping algorithms.

> Counting actual paths on a complete small grid

Take all ordered pairs of the 49 integer points in [−2,4]², including equal
endpoints and reversals, against viewport [0,2]². That is 49²=2401 directed
segments. A trace with k cuts has k+1 decisions and 2+k outcode evaluations.
Each decision performs the OR test; an accepting final decision skips the AND
test. The next program checks these identities for every segment and counts
accepted/rejected paths by actual cut count. This is controlled geometric data;
no external dataset is needed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cohen_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::array<std::size_t,5> accepted{},rejected{};
  std::size_t total=0,decisions=0,cuts=0;
  for(int x0=-2;x0<=4;++x0)for(int y0=-2;y0<=4;++y0)
  for(int x1=-2;x1<=4;++x1)for(int y1=-2;y1<=4;++y1) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip_trace(x0,y0,x1,y1,0,0,2,2);
    if(t.intersections>4 || t.steps.size()!=t.intersections+1 || t.outcode_evaluations!=t.intersections+2 || t.or_tests!=t.steps.size() || t.and_tests!=t.steps.size()-t.result.visible)return 1;
    (t.result.visible?accepted:rejected)[t.intersections]++;
    ++total;decisions+=t.steps.size();cuts+=t.intersections;
  }
  if(total!=2401)return 1;
  std::vector<double> x_yes,x_no,y_yes,y_no;
  std::print("| actual edge cuts | accepted | rejected |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<5;++k) {
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",k,accepted[k],rejected[k]);
    x_yes.push_back(k-.18);x_no.push_back(k+.18);y_yes.push_back(accepted[k]);y_no.push_back(rejected[k]);
  }
  std::print("\nTotal directed segments {}; actual cuts {}; decisions {}.\n",total,cuts,decisions);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.bars(x_yes,y_yes,"accepted",{22,163,74},.32).bars(x_no,y_no,"rejected",{220,38,38},.32)
      .legend({"accepted","rejected"},{{22,163,74},{220,38,38}})
      .title("All 2401 directed grid segments: actual edge cuts")
      .x_label("number of intersection computations").y_label("number of directed segments")
      .x_limits(-.6,4.6).y_limits(0,1100).size(900,580)
      .save_svg("build/cohen_work.svg");
}
```
