// result_end
\caption(Actual trace counters separate trivial decisions from edge arithmetic. Four directed segments attain the four-cut bound; zero-height rejected bars remain visible as absent work categories.)
\alttext(Green accepted and red rejected segment counts are grouped by zero through four actual edge cuts, for every ordered pair in a seven-by-seven integer grid.)

> Closed contacts, validation and numerical limits

The rectangle may have zero width, zero height or both. A point on it is
visible. A line tangent to a corner can return two equal visible endpoints.
All eight endpoint/bound coordinates are validated before trivial decisions:
NaN/infinity and reversed bounds throw invalid_argument. This prevents a NaN
from being classified as inside merely because its less-than and greater-than
comparisons are both false. The output layout remains bool plus four doubles;
a rejected result has four zero coordinates and should be read using visible.

The last program checks an exact corner touch, one-ULP cases on either side,
degenerate rectangles, invalid input, subnormal values and a huge diagonal.
The huge diagonal is asserted only when long double has a wider exponent range;
otherwise an unrepresentable widened difference throws overflow_error, even
though the original double endpoints are finite. The trace owns at most five
records; the output-only call needs no heap allocation or shared mutable state.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::array<double,3> heights{std::nextafter(2.,0.),2.,std::nextafter(2.,3.)};
  const std::array<std::string_view,3> names{"one ULP below","exact touch","one ULP above"};
  std::print("| upper endpoint height | native visibility |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<heights.size();++i) {
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(-1,0,1,heights[i],0,0,1,1);
    if(c.visible!=(i<2))return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",names[i],c.visible);
  }
  const auto touch=dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(-1,0,1,2,0,0,1,1);
  if(touch.x0!=0 || touch.y0!=1 || touch.x1!=0 || touch.y1!=1)return 1;
  const auto point=dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(-3,-3,0,0,-1,-1,-1,-1);
  if(!point.visible || point.x0!=-1 || point.y0!=-1 || point.x1!=-1 || point.y1!=-1)return 1;
  const double tiny=std::numeric_limits<double>::denorm_min();
  const auto small=dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(-tiny,0,tiny,0,0,0,tiny,tiny);
  if(!small.visible || small.x0!=0 || small.x1!=tiny)return 1;
  bool invalid=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN(),0,0,0,-1,-1,1,1);}
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&){invalid=true;}
  if(!invalid)return 1;
  std::print("\nExact corner and point-rectangle contacts pass. Subnormal crossing passes. NaN input throws invalid_argument.\n");
  const double maximum=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
  if(std::numeric_limits<long double>::max_exponent>std::numeric_limits<double>::max_exponent) {
    const auto c=dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(-maximum,-maximum,maximum,maximum,-1,-1,1,1);
    if(!c.visible || c.x0!=-1 || c.y0!=-1 || c.x1!=1 || c.y1!=1)return 1;
    std::print("Huge original diagonal clips to ({},{}) to ({},{}).\n",c.x0,c.y0,c.x1,c.y1);
  } else {
    bool overflow=false;
    try{(void)dm::algorithms::cohen_sutherland_clip(-maximum,-maximum,maximum,maximum,-1,-1,1,1);}
    catch(const std::overflow_error&){overflow=true;}
    if(!overflow)return 1;
    std::print("Huge endpoint differences exceed this platform's working format.\n");
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| upper endpoint height | native visibility |
| :--- | :--- |
| one ULP below | true |
| exact touch | true |
| one ULP above | false |

Exact corner and point-rectangle contacts pass. Subnormal crossing passes. NaN input throws invalid_argument.
Huge original diagonal clips to (-1,-1) to (1,1).
// result_end
\caption(Actual checks retain closed contacts, distinguish the one-ULP miss without a hidden tolerance and avoid the former huge product/difference overflow.)

These checks establish specific controls, rather than universal exactness.
Finite arithmetic can round a determinant, alter a near-corner classification
or lose detail at extreme scale separations. Current-range projection preserves
termination and prior half-planes, but does not prove exact collinearity with
the original real line. Applications needing certified geometric predicates
must use an exact or adaptive construction. The reported intersection and
outcode counters exclude validation, snapshot copies and runtime costs.

> Conclusion

The original horizontal and oblique crossings give the same four visible
endpoints, while trivial inside/outside cases avoid edge arithmetic. Actual
records expose an important inconclusive case: a left-only endpoint can acquire
a top bit before rejection. Termination follows from permanently satisfied
half-planes, giving a worst-case four cuts, rather than from a monotonically
decreasing endpoint bit count. Native counters, exact small controls and
explicit numerical limits make the implementation's behavior inspectable.

> References

- A. Kriegl (2003), [Cohen–Sutherland Line-Clipping Algorithm](https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~kriegl/Skripten/CG/node31.html), University of Vienna. Teaching notes specify the outcode convention, convex acceptance and shared-half-plane rejection used here.
- C++ working draft, [Linear interpolation](https://eel.is/c++draft/c.math.lerp). The kernel uses its finite interpolation behavior as a fallback when widened determinant products cannot be represented.
